    <div class="card">
      <h3>Key Findings</h3>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">1</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Rent Burden Is a Universal Crisis — Not Confined to Low-Income Cities</h4>
          <p>The global mean rent-to-salary ratio of 61.5% signals a cross-income-level housing affordability crisis. Remarkably, the highest rent burdens are found in wealthy cities: Seattle (71.2%), Zurich (70.8%), Geneva (70.2%), Lausanne (70.1%), and Boston (69.9%). 27 of 80 cities exceed 65% — meaning a third of all analyzed cities have structurally unsustainable housing markets. Policy interventions focused solely on "poor" cities will miss the majority of the affected population.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">2</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Tier 5 Cities Face a Compound Crisis: Low Wages + High Inflation + Oil Dependency</h4>
          <p>The 14 Tier 5 cities average $455/month in salaries against a 28.4% average inflation rate. Crucially, 11 of these 14 cities have High oil shock exposure, indicating that external commodity price shocks amplify domestic inflation in structurally vulnerable economies. Buenos Aires (150%), Beirut (50%), Cairo (28%), and Lagos (34%) require immediate attention as hyperinflationary conditions erode real wages faster than any housing subsidy can compensate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">3</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>South Asia Hosts the Largest Concentration of Severe Affordability Crises</h4>
          <p>South Asia has 8 cities in the dataset, with 5 classified Tier 5 (Severe) — the highest concentration of any region. Average salary in the region is $553/month with 53.6% average rent burden and 8.4% average inflation. Mumbai, Delhi, Dhaka, Karachi, and Lahore collectively represent over 100 million urban residents facing severe affordability conditions. India alone (Mumbai, Delhi, Bangalore) spans Tiers 4–5 despite rapid GDP growth, indicating urban wage gains are not translating into affordability improvements.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">4</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>The Purchasing Power Gap of 148.7 Index Points Reflects Extreme Global Inequality</h4>
          <p>Basel's PPP index of 183.7 versus Beirut's 35.0 spans nearly the entire range of economic capacity. Tier 1 cities average PPP of 157.1 versus Tier 5 average of 48.0 — a 3.3× differential. This means residents in the most affluent cities have more than 3× the effective purchasing power of those in crisis cities, even before accounting for differences in social safety nets or currency volatility.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">5</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Oil Shock Exposure Is the Strongest Predictor of Inflation Vulnerability</h4>
          <p>Cities with High oil exposure average 20.4% inflation versus 3.4% for Low-exposure cities — a 6× differential. This is the largest structural predictor in the dataset. High-exposure cities are concentrated in South Asia, West Africa, and the Middle East conflict zones. Energy transition policies and fuel-price subsidy frameworks are likely more impactful than direct cost-of-living interventions in these markets.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">6</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Western Europe and North America Drive High RTS% Despite High Wages</h4>
          <p>Western Europe averages 66.9% RTS and North America 67.0% — the two highest regional rent burdens globally — despite having the highest average salaries ($3,200–$6,800/month range). This indicates rent inflation has consistently outpaced wage growth in advanced economies. The dominant crisis label in North America is "High Cost – High Wages," suggesting structural supply constraints in housing rather than income inadequacy as the primary driver.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

