    <div class="card">
      <h3>Areas for Further Research</h3>
      <ul style="font-size:0.875rem;line-height:2;padding-left:20px;">
        <li><strong>Informal economy adjustments:</strong> Many Tier 4–5 cities have significant informal sectors; official salary figures likely understate true consumption capacity and overstate relative affordability.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporal tracking:</strong> Single-year snapshots obscure trends. Longitudinal data would identify which cities are converging toward or diverging from crisis conditions fastest.</li>
        <li><strong>Subsidy/social transfer effects:</strong> The dataset reflects market prices without adjusting for housing vouchers, rent controls, or social benefits that materially alter effective affordability in some cities.</li>
        <li><strong>Within-city inequality:</strong> City-level averages mask extreme distributional effects; median and percentile data would better capture the lived experience of lower-income urban residents.</li>
